  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 AM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWhile the \"main event\" of our precipitation has wrapped up, the \nsecond round of it is moving into the area early this morning as our \ndeep upper-level low dropping through eastern Oregon today continues \nto bring up Pacific moisture from the remnants of Rachel. This low \nis going to move south into Nevada and then east through Utah \nthrough the first half of this week, keeping rain chances around not \nonly today, but also on Tuesday and Wednesday. Rain chances remain \nwidespread today, but the higher rainfall amounts are expected to be \nconcentrated in parts of the South Hills and Eastern Highlands and \nwill likely range from 0.20-0.40 inches of rainfall with some \nlocally higher amounts. That said, we are still expecting some \ncontinued rainfall throughout the rest of our CWA, too, so the Flood \nWatch will continue as expected. By this afternoon, shower coverage \nis expected to break up a bit and become a bit more spotty. On the \ntopic of current alerts, a Frost Advisory and Freeze Warning \ncontinue this morning as temperatures continue to drop into the 30s \nin the eastern Magic Valley, Snake River Plain, and out across the \nArco Desert area. Expect a mix of freeze warnings and frost \nadvisories to continue for the next several mornings, especially \nwith Tuesday and Wednesday mornings looking even colder. Of course, \nthe cloud cover could keep temperatures from dropping quite as much \nas we expect, but we will see where they end up this morning and go \nfrom there. Of note, our Frost/Freeze products generally end on \nOctober 15th. \n\nHighs today will struggle to make it into the 50s for \nmost and even where it does, we'll only reach the mid 50s. The \nCentral Mountains will slowly begin to warm up on Tuesday as \nprecipitation begins to move out wit highs reaching the 50s there \nand upper 40s to low 50s everywhere else. In the second half of the \nweek, we see high pressure beginning to build back in over Idaho, so \nlook forward to a warming, drying trend. By Thursday afternoon, more \nof us will return to the 60s. Given the current forecast, next \nweekend looks like a beautiful fall weekend to get out and\nenjoy.",
